course: dictionary
lesson: 7612
title: Privacy: keeping things to yourself
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Privacy means being away from other people, or keeping things about you safe from people who should not see or know them. When you meet a new word, read the words around it. «Everyone also deserves privacy: their own room, their diary, their messages and their time in the bathroom.» A diary, messages and the bathroom are all things you keep to yourself, so privacy means having your own space and your own things kept to yourself.
explain: Privacy also covers personal information online. «His dad blocks and reports the account and turns on the privacy settings.» Privacy settings are controls that stop strangers from seeing your photos, messages and details. Ask yourself: what would fit in the gap? Try "keeping things to yourself" in the sentence instead of the hard word. If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
explain: Privacy is an uncountable noun, so we say "more privacy" or "some privacy", not "two privacies". Some people do not have much privacy, and others are said to "respect someone's privacy" when they leave that person's things alone.
fact: Privacy means being away from other people so they cannot see or hear you.
fact: Privacy also means keeping personal information safe, such as your address, passwords or photos.
fact: «Privacy» is uncountable: you say more privacy, not many privacies.
fact: Useful phrases: privacy settings, respect someone's privacy, in privacy.
fact: Never share your full name, home address, school or passwords with people online.
exercise: «His dad turns on the privacy settings.» What are privacy settings for? | Turning up the volume on a phone | Choosing a new game | Making the screen brighter | *Controlling who can see your photos and messages
exercise: Which information should you keep private online? | Your favourite colour | The weather today | Your favourite animal | *Your home address
exercise: «Some fights are about privacy.» Find and copy one word that means keeping things to yourself. | about | Some | *privacy | fights
test: «Young famous people deserve even more privacy, because they are still children.» Why do young famous people deserve even more privacy? | Because they have more money | Because they are older than most people | *Because they are still children | Because they like being filmed
test: Which of these is an invasion of privacy? | Lending a friend a pencil | Saying hello to a neighbour | Sharing a funny joke with your class | *Reading someone's private diary without asking
exam: «People who did not agree to be filmed have their faces blurred.» Why are their faces blurred? | To hide the colour of the sky | To make the people look funnier | To make the film look older | *To protect their privacy
